    </DevTodo>

    <p>
        Cmdr is keyboard-first, and it follows your macOS accessibility settings. This page lists what works today, how
        to turn it on, what's still missing, and how to get help. For a criterion-by-criterion view (WCAG 2.2, Section
        508, EN 301 549), see the <a href="/trust/accessibility">accessibility conformance report</a>.
    </p>

    <nav aria-label="On this page">
        <ul>
            {
                toc.map((item) => (
                    <li>
                        <a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a>
                    </li>
                ))
            }
        </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="keyboard">Use Cmdr from the keyboard</h2>
    <p>Everything core in Cmdr works without a mouse.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Every action has a shortcut</strong>, and you can change any of them in Settings &gt; Keyboard
            shortcuts. Help &gt; Keyboard shortcuts lists them all in one window.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Command palette</strong>: press ⌘⇧P (Command-Shift-P) and type to find any command by name.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Switch panes</strong>: press Tab to move between the left and right file lists.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Reach the pane controls</strong>: press ⌃F5 (Control-F5) to move focus to the focused pane's tabs,
            location bar, and sort buttons. Arrow keys move between them, and Escape takes you back to the files. New in
            0.53.0.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Reach the notifications</strong>: press ⌃F6 (Control-F6) to move focus to the notifications and
            their buttons, like Undo. Arrow keys move between them, and Escape takes you back. New in 0.53.0.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Undo a move to trash</strong> any time from Edit &gt; Undo move to trash, after its notification is
            gone. New in 0.53.0.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Full Keyboard Access</strong>: in the main window, Tab switches panes, so macOS Full Keyboard Access
            doesn't move through the pane controls. Use ⌃F5 for those. Menus are native macOS menus, so they work as
            usual.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="screen-readers">Screen readers</h2>
    <p>
        Cmdr works with VoiceOver through the macOS accessibility interface, and VoiceOver's own keyboard commands pass
        through.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Works today</strong>: dialogs, Settings, and forms are built for screen readers, and dialogs keep
            and return focus correctly. Menus are native, so VoiceOver reads them like any other
            app's. Notifications are read out without moving your focus (new in 0.53.0).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Not yet</strong>: the file list doesn't tell VoiceOver which file the cursor is on or which files
            are marked (<a href={issueUrl(437)}>#437</a>). That's the biggest gap.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Still to test</strong>: a full hands-on VoiceOver test is pending. The
            <a href="/trust/accessibility#pending">conformance report</a> lists what it covers and gets updated with
            the results.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="seeing">Text size, zoom, and colors</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Text size</strong>: set it from 75% to 150% in Settings &gt; Appearance &gt; Zoom and density &gt;
            Text size, or with ⌘+ and ⌘− (Command-plus and Command-minus; ⌘0 resets it). It scales the whole app, rows
            included, and adds to the macOS text size in System Settings &gt; Accessibility, so text can go past 200%.
        </li>
        <li><strong>macOS Zoom</strong> works with Cmdr like with any other app.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Light and dark mode</strong>: Settings &gt; Appearance &gt; Theme mode. Pick Light, Dark, or
            System to follow macOS.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>System font and accent color</strong>: Cmdr uses the macOS system font and your accent color.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Contrast</strong>: text contrast is checked automatically in light and dark mode, with every macOS
            accent color. Turn on Increase contrast in System Settings &gt; Accessibility &gt; Display, and Cmdr
            draws stronger borders, secondary text, focus rings, and pane tints.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Differentiate without color</strong>: turn it on in the same place, and a bar marks the focused
            pane and each marked file, so you don't have to tell them apart by color.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="motion">Motion and transparency</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Reduce motion</strong>: turn it on in System Settings &gt; Accessibility &gt; Display, and Cmdr's
            activity indicators, like spinners, stop moving. Cmdr has no flashing content, and no audio or video.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Reduce transparency</strong>: turn it on in the same place, and Cmdr's frosted-glass surfaces turn
            solid.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="notifications">Time to read notifications</h2>
    <p>
        Notifications go away after a few seconds by default. To keep them up until you close them, turn on Settings
        &gt; Behavior &gt; Notifications &gt; "Keep notifications until I close them" (new in 0.53.0). A notification
        also stays while your pointer or keyboard focus is on it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gaps">Known gaps</h2>
    <p>
        Cmdr doesn't fully meet WCAG 2.2 Level AA yet. These open issues track what's missing, and each one is public,
        so you can follow the fix:
    </p>
    <ul>
        {
            gaps.map((issue) => (
                <li>
                    <a href={issueUrl(issue.number)}>#{issue.number}</a>: {issue.title}
                </li>
            ))
        }
    </ul>

    <h2 id="help">Get help</h2>
    <p>
        Need something to work differently, or hit a wall? Write to
        <a href="mailto:hello@getcmdr.com">hello@getcmdr.com</a>, or use Help &gt; Send feedback in the app.
        Accessibility questions and reports are welcome, and every one gets an answer. If something blocks you, say
        what you use (for example VoiceOver, Zoom, or a switch device) and what you tried, so the fix can target it.
    </p>
</LegalLayout>
